explosions have reportedly hit a Russian  Air Base far from the Ukrainian border  damaging two long-range bombers  unverified video of the blasts have been  posted on a variety of telegram channels  the blasts early on Monday  struck the air base near the city of  Engels more than 600 kilometers inside  of Russian territory the bombers damaged  are said to be of the same type used to  carry out air strikes on Ukraine's  infrastructure another explosion hit a  military base near the Russian city of  rayazon at least three people were  reportedly killed after a fuel tank  exploded so far there has been no claim  of responsibility for either of these  blasts so let's get more on this we are  joined by our correspondent Nick  Connelly who is standing by for us Nick  tell us a little bit more about what we  know about the explosions  yep so so far the kind of evidence we  have to go on is pretty thin we've seen  images on social media of basically some  kind of closed circuit TV cameras from  houses near That Base you just kind of  hear a big bang and some of the other  footages you see a kind of explosion of  kind of flash of light on the kind of  night sky but we have heard in the case  of Ingles from the regional Governor  telling civilians not to panic saying  pointedly that civilian infrastructure  hadn't been affected and wasn't going to  be affected so implying that definitely  there had been some kind of attack on  the military base there which is  basically one of the biggest Strategic  Air bases in Russia to where some of the  bombers that in the threat if nuclear  war became a real possibility they would  be launched from there from from planes  from that base and we've seen that base  being used to then fly uh missiles that  are aiming at Ukraine to the Caspian Sea  and launch them from so there so this is  a real kind of direct cause and effect  if indeed this is Ukraine going after  the kind of very same planes that have  been wreaking such havoc on Ukraine as  for design the other base there where we  have that Russian uh official line that  this was an explosion of a petrol tanker  but you know not many people here  believe that they say that this is  basically just a kind of fig Leaf an  attempt to kind of explain away another  Ukrainian attack whether these were  missiles or drones we don't know yet  here in Ukraine everyone is kind of  convinced that these were chemikaze  drones tell us a little bit more about  uh the response of the Ukrainian and the  Russian authorities  well so far we the Ukrainian kind of  government sources are keeping quiet  some of the kind of military bloggers  and social media accounts associated  with Ukraine's military have been  reporting on this with lots of different  Smileys and kind of ironic uh texts  accompanying these images  um I think it's unlikely that you know  Ukraine would ever kind of directly own  up to this as for the Russian side well  we've seen time and time again when  things go badly for the Russian army on  the battlefield they try to basically  keep quiet for as long as possible until  they basically make up some kind of  face-saving explanation the same was the  case when Ukraine sunk Russia's most  important battleship in the Black Sea  back in the summer took days for them to  really kind of work out what their line  would be so I think there's little uh  kind of expectation that we're going to  hear something soon but this is really  big news this is the first time that  Ukraine if it is indeed Ukraine has  attempted these kind of things that deep  in Russian territory that far away from  the front lines and it shows that  Russia's air defense just aren't up to  the task and that Ukraine is now able to  make Russia feel as vulnerable as  Ukraine itself has been feeling  vulnerable in recent weeks and months I  think psychologically I think it's  difficult to overestimate the effect of  this for ordinary Russians to see that  Ukraine is not just the place where War  happens but the Ukraine is also  potentially able to bring War to Russian  cities let's talk a little bit more  about the vulnerability on the Ukrainian  side because it's been experiencing  severe energy shortage because of the  Russian airstrikes how is the situation  where you are now  so we are now quite some time from the  last kind of major attack these Mass  missile attacks that we've been seeing  time and time again in recent months and  things are slowly getting back to normal  it's not perfect until you still have  kind of emergency blackouts when the  system is overloaded but broadly things  are getting better it is taking longer  each time for Ukraine to recover because  just basically the spare parts are  running out and the foreign help isn't  getting here fast enough but there is a  sense here in Ukraine there is also just  little point in rebuilding every time  the Russians decide to destroy this  infrastructure that actually it is now  more worthwhile going after these  Targets in Russia to prevent Russia  launching these kind of attacks but  there just aren't enough trans  Transformers enough in US equipment you  know on the world markets to keep on  rebuilding every week and you know we've  still got four or five months of winter  to go so definitely a kind of change in  mood here Nick Connelly in the Ukrainian  Capital Kiev thank you so much
 

